agent/answer.py:27-33

    def run_tool(decision: dict, session: dict) -> str:
        """Executes the tool the model asked for."""
        if decision["tool"] == "refund":
            return issue_refund(decision["account_id"], decision["amount"])
        if decision["tool"] == "lookup":
            return lookup(decision["account_id"])
        return "unknown tool"

Both the operation and its object come out of `decision`, the model's own output. `session` is accepted on line 27 and never read — the authenticated identity was in the function's hands and was dropped. `issue_refund` and `lookup` (answer.py:59-64) take the account id as a plain string and act on it; no role check, no object-level authorization and no downstream token appears on the path. No tool with an irreversible effect is behind a confirmation gate anywhere in the target (AI-07: the set of side-effecting tools is {issue_refund, summarize_and_run, remember_from_reply}; the set of gated tools is empty).

config/mcp.json:3-7

    "billing": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@acme/billing-mcp@latest"],
      "env": { "BILLING_API_KEY": "[REDACTED:secret]" }
    }

The value is a literal, not a reference. Format class recorded rather than reproduced: vendor prefix `sk-acme-` followed by 32 lowercase hexadecimal characters, 40 characters in total. It was not tested against any provider, per the engagement contract. The sibling entry at config/mcp.json:11 sets `"DOCS_TOKEN": "${DOCS_TOKEN}"`, which establishes that the substitution idiom was available in this very file and was not used for the billing key.
